Rem ===================================================================
Rem cave-ask.shoddy -- reading a line, and cutting it into words.
Rem 
Rem misc.c:230-300. Words are significant to five letters, as they have
Rem been since 1977. When stdin is not a terminal the C echoes each
Rem command back, which is what makes a transcript read as a dialogue.
Rem ===================================================================

Rem ---- input -------------------------------------------------------------

Rem At end of input there is no command to echo, so the prompt is left
Rem hanging exactly as the C leaves it before the final score.
Rem End of input is not the same as an empty line, and the difference
Rem decides whether to reprompt or to go and score the game. InputLine
Rem reports it separately; this lifts its flat carrier into a proper answer.
Type Line = Eof | Typed(String)

Rem Two entry points, because the C has two. A yes/no prompt calls
Rem get_input directly, so a blank line reaches it and earns "Please answer
Rem the question."; a command prompt goes through get_command_input, which
Rem throws blank lines away and reads again (misc.c:566-572).
Rem main.c:1493-1494 -- -o drops the prompt string, leaving only the
Rem blank line get_input prints before every read.
Def OldStyleMode() As Boolean
    Contains(Args(), "-o")

Def Prompt() As String
    If OldStyleMode() Then
        "\n"
    Else
        "\n> "

Def Ask() As Line
    Skipping(AskRaw())

Def Skipping(l As Line) As Line
    Select Case l
        Case Typed(s)
            If Len(s) = 0 Then
                Skipping(AskRaw())
            Else
                l
        Case Else
            l

Def AskRaw() As Line
    AskLine(InputLine(Prompt()))

Rem misc.c:251-254. A line beginning with "#" is swallowed whole: no echo,
Rem no reply, and -- because the blank line is printed once before the read
Rem loop rather than inside it -- no second prompt either. The recorded test
Rem scripts are full of these, which is how they carry their own commentary.
Rem misc.c:566-572 then throws away a blank line and reads another, so a
Rem stray newline mid-script costs a prompt and nothing else.
Def AskLine(r As Array) As Line
    If Nth(r, 1) Then
        Eof()
    Else
        Let s = Nth(r, 2)
        If Len(s) > 0 And Left(s, 1) = "#" Then
            AskLine(InputLine(""))
        Else
            Print(s)
            Typed(s)

Def LineText(l As Line) As String
    Select Case l
        Case Typed(s)
            s
        Case Else
            ""

Rem misc.c:275-360. "yes" and "y" both match on their own length, so
Rem "yesterday" is a yes; that quirk is older than most of its players.
Def YesNo(question As String, yes As String, no As String) As Boolean
    Speak(question)
    Answered(AskRaw(), question, yes, no)

Rem misc.c:281-287 gives up only at end of input. A blank line is not that:
Rem yes_or_no reads through get_input rather than get_command_input, so
Rem nothing swallows it, and it earns the same scolding as any other answer
Rem that is neither yes nor no.
Def Answered(l As Line, question As String, yes As String, no As String) As Boolean
    Select Case l
        Case Eof
            False
        Case Typed(raw)
            Replied(Lower(Trim(raw)), question, yes, no)

Def Replied(reply As String, question As String, yes As String, no As String) As Boolean
    If Len(reply) = 0 Then
        SpeakId(MsgPleaseAnswer())
        YesNo(question, yes, no)
    Else
        Let w = First(Tokens(reply))
        If StartsWith("yes", w) Or StartsWith("y", w) Then
            Speak(yes)
            True
        Else
            If StartsWith("no", w) Or StartsWith("n", w) Then
                Speak(no)
                False
            Else
                SpeakId(MsgPleaseAnswer())
                YesNo(question, yes, no)

